Ladybird now queues a media element’s pending tasks again for the
document that adopts it, so a load that’s under way carries on there.
Gecko behaves the same way: Its HTMLMediaElement::QueueTask() and
RunInStableState() dispatch to the main thread, not to a document.
Blink instead restarts a load that’s under way when the element moves
to another document, in HTMLMediaElement::AttachToNewFrame(), which
DidMoveToNewDocument() calls.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79 and Firefox 151.0: For a video whose src
was set in an iframe, adopted, and whose iframe was then removed,
Firefox loads it, as Ladybird now does, and Chrome never does. With a
failed candidate’s task still pending when the old document goes away,
Firefox loads the next source, as Ladybird does, and Chrome never does;
with the old document kept, both load it. And for a round trip through
a document that isn’t fully active, Firefox fires error at the failed
candidate and loads the next source, as Ladybird now does, while Chrome
restarts and loads the source inserted ahead of the candidate.
